            "source_entity_instance": "MOSES",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "\u201ctwo-patch\u201d approach",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In MOSES a \"two-patch\" approach is used in which the canopy is modelled by conceptually placing it beside bare ground and calculating entirely separate energy balances for bare ground and vegetation, hence neglecting radiative and aerodynamic interaction between the two systems and their mediation of each others' microclimate.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MOSES",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Four soil layers to a depth of 3.0 m",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Soil temperature and moisture are calculated for four soil layers to a depth of 3.0 m in MOSES and six layers to a depth of 4.6 m in CABLE.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "two-leaf canopy model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The CABLE model (v1.8) has been coupled to the UM and is used in ACCESS1.1 simulations. CABLE is a one-layer two-leaf canopy model as described in Wang and Leuning (1998), and was formulated on the basis of the multilayer model of Leuning (1995).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Turbulent transport within the canopy based on localized near-field theory",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Turbulent transport within the canopy based on localized near-field theory (Raupach, 1989), and transport just above the canopy in the roughness sublayer (RSL) is simulated. The inclusion of a representation of the RSL is critical to the performance of CABLE.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "diurnally resolved canopy albedo",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The canopy albedo is resolved diurnally as a function of beam fraction, the sun angle, canopy leaf area index, leaf angle distribution, and the transmittance and reflectance of the leaves.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Six layers, total depth of 4.6 m",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Soil temperature and moisture are calculated for four soil layers to a depth of 3.0 m in MOSES and six layers to a depth of 4.6 m in CABLE.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Subsurface tiling",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Subsurface tiling is used in CABLE, where each surface tile has a corresponding soil tile for the calculation of soil temperature, moisture, and runoff, while in MOSES the soil is common to all tiles within a grid cell.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "prognostic snow density",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In CABLE, the snow metamorphism and the bulk snow properties are accounted for through changes in snow density; see Gordon et al. (2002). In CABLE the density of the fresh snow is 120 kg m\u22123, and with time it may increase to 400 kg m\u22123, while in MOSES it remains constant at 250 kg m\u22123.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CABLE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "variable snow thermal conductivity",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In CABLE, thermal conductivity for new snow is 0.2 Wm\u22122 K\u22121 and increases with snow density up to 0.5 W m\u22122 K\u22121, while in MOSES it remains constant at 0.265 W m\u22122 K\u22121.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Unified Model (UM)",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "HadGEM2(r1.1) atmospheric physics",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The atmospheric component of ACCESS (Bi et al., 2013) used in these simulations is the UK Met Office UM with HadGEM2(r1.1) atmospheric physics as described in Davies et al. (2005) and The HadGEM2 Development Team et al. (2011).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ACCESS1.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "atmosphere-only",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "We perform atmosphere-only simulations following the Atmosphere Model Intercomparison Project (AMIP) experimental design with prescribed sea surface temperature and sea ice to constrain the climatology and aid in interpreting the differences between ACCESS1.0 (MOSES) and ACCESS1.1 (CABLE) simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ACCESS1.1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "atmosphere-only",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "We perform atmosphere-only simulations following the Atmosphere Model Intercomparison Project (AMIP) experimental design with prescribed sea surface temperature and sea ice to constrain the climatology and aid in interpreting the differences between ACCESS1.0 (MOSES) and ACCESS1.1 (CABLE) simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
